WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:04.360
Every image you've seen of a black hole,

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:06.800
including this one, is a fantasy.

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:09.940
A genuine picture of a black hole doesn't exist,

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:12.980
but soon, that's going to change.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.680
Black holes in their own don't look like very much.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.380
They don't reflect light

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:22.220
because any light that falls into them can never escape.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:24.960
And they don't emit any light that we can detect

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:26.920
Those aren't the only reasons black holes

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:28.560
are hard to photograph.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:31.360
Even though they're really, really massive —

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:33.440
they can be millions or billions of times

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:34.820
the mass of the Sun

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:36.940
— they don't take up that much space on the sky.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.540
They've compressed all that mass

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:40.540
into a really tiny, little space.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:43.660
The closest big black hole to Earth

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:45.460
is at the centre of the Milky Way,

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:48.020
which is 26,000 light-years away from us.

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:52.460
And so in order to be able to see something of that size,

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.900
we need a telescope that has a resolution that is

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:57.460
powerful enough that it could see

00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:59.500
a doughnut on the surface of the moon.

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:02.880
The black hole illustrations we're all familiar with

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.480
aren't complete lies.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:07.700
Plenty of data suggest that black holes exist.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:10.880
Some even shoot jets into space that we can see

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:13.660
and spaghettify stars that get too close.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:15.880
The best evidence for these behemoths

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:17.260
is their entourage.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:21.980
They wrap huge amounts of gas

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:24.600
and like charged plasma around them.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.600
And as that stuff is trying to fall into the black hole,

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:28.960
it can't all go in at once,

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.140
so it orbits super, super, super fast.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:35.160
And the friction in that fast orbiting heats it white-hot.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:38.660
Most illustrations focus on the accretion disk,

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:41.640
but soon, we could be looking at the real deal —

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:46.240
thanks to the Event Horizon Telescope or the EHT.

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:48.780
The EHT is trying to take the first picture

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:51.320
of the black hole at the center of the Milky Way.

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:55.260
It's called Sagittarius A* or Sgr A* for short.

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:58.440
And the team expects to produce results later this year.

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.260
What it's actually doing is taking a picture of

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:03.720
the shadow of the black hole on the stuff behind it.

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:07.360
By stuff, she means the glowing gas and plasma

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.060
in the accretion disk.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:10.760
The black hole's shadow should create

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:13.060
a silhouette on the disk that outlines

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:16.180
the point of no-return — the event horizon.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:18.900
Taking such a picture would require

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.240
a really, really big telescope.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:25.700
The EHT uses a lot of smaller radio telescopes instead.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:31.220
They work together using a technique called

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:33.540
very long baseline interferometry

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:35.320
and what that does is it makes those

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:38.000
telescopes kind of act like one giant telescope.

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:42.420
Scientists analyzing the data can see

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:45.140
how the light waves detected by each telescope

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:47.000
line up with each other to figure out

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.320
what's light from the edge of the black hole

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:51.020
and what's noise.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:54.820
The EHT's picture will most likely look like a ring,

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:57.060
created by the black hole's event horizon

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:59.060
blocking out light from behind.

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:01.520
That first image, though perhaps grainy,

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:04.560
will give us an up-close look at the accretion disk

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.760
plus two key bits of information:

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:10.220
the size and shape of the black hole's shadow.

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:14.000
And then that will be enough to answer

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.940
a lot of interesting astrophysical questions

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:17.240
about how black holes work,

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:18.680
how event horizons work

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:20.140
and what is going on there.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:22.980
There the big unanswered questions include:

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:25.280
How exactly do accretion discs

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:27.280
form and feed black holes?

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.760
Where do black hole jets originate?

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:32.260
And the oldie but always a goodie,

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:34.260
was Einstein right?

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:36.500
His general theory of relativity predicted that

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:40.060
event horizons could conceal tiny, massive objects

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:41.280
like black holes.

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:45.060
The shape of this image that we see is something

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.500
that you can predict using general relativity.

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:51.140
If the shape of the image of the black hole

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:53.080
looks different than we expect,

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:55.520
that would mean that something's wrong

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:58.900
that we don't fully understand about general relativity.

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:01.540
Taking a picture of a black hole will mark

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:04.940
the most extreme test of general relativity yet.

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:07.800
This test of relativity also has implications for

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:10.680
the physics theory of it deals with very small objects,

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.520
quantum mechanics.

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:14.920
The two of them don't play well together.

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:17.140
We don't understand how gravity works

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:18.800
on sort of quantum levels.

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:21.740
And if we see something that differs from our

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:24.640
expectation in this picture of the black hole,

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:28.400
that can help point us to one of many theories

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:31.120
that try to reconcile quantum mechanics

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:32.820
and general relativity.

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:35.040
It's also just cool.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:36.680
Whether it's a pale blue dot

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:39.120
or a pristine portrait of Pluto,

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:41.240
seeing has always offered us

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:43.640
a new perspective on the universe.

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:45.420
Humans are very visual,

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:47.060
and so we love having images.

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:49.540
And I think having an actual picture of black hole

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:52.600
is just going to blow people's minds.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:54.300
Am I going to like break continuity

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:55.540
if I roll up my sleeves?

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:56.500
And I'm going to start this one over.

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:57.380
This is hard.

00:04:57.740 --> 00:04:58.460
I was just watching the

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:00.100
Great British Baking Show last night.

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:01.160
Ummm.

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:02.460
They wear the same clothes

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:03.620
two days in a row.

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:04.900
We'll go there, yeah.

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:06.400
I feel like it's wrong.

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:08.400
Sorry, yeah, you can cut that.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:10.660
So, don't roll up your sleeves then.

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:12.940
Something like that. [Laughs]

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:15.080
[Laughing]

